Q: Cartesian product of std::tuple A: <p>Here is another proposal without recursion that generalizes the cartesian product up to N tuples.</p>
<p>For illustration, we use in the following only two tuples with 2 and 3 types, e.g.:</p>
<pre><code>using A = std::tuple&lt;int,char&gt;;
using B = std::tuple&lt;double,short,std::string&gt;;
</code></pre>
<p>so the cartesian product should have 6 entries. Now if we are able to generate the indexes that tells which element to take from each tuple, we have done most of the job. In the example, we could generate the following &quot;coordinates tuple&quot;:</p>
<pre><code>std::tuple&lt;
    std::integer_sequence&lt;std::size_t,0,0&gt;,
    std::integer_sequence&lt;std::size_t,0,1&gt;,
    std::integer_sequence&lt;std::size_t,0,2&gt;,
    std::integer_sequence&lt;std::size_t,1,0&gt;,
    std::integer_sequence&lt;std::size_t,1,1&gt;,
    std::integer_sequence&lt;std::size_t,1,2&gt;
&gt;
</code></pre>
<p>For doing this, we define a <code>Coords</code> template class (using a similar approach than <a href="https://stackoverflow.com/a/79020459/2229039">this post</a>)</p>
<pre><code>template&lt;typename...Tuples&gt;
struct Coords {

    static constexpr std::size_t N = sizeof...(Tuples);
    static constexpr std::size_t K = (1*...*std::tuple_size_v&lt;Tuples&gt;);

    template&lt;std::size_t...Is&gt;
    static constexpr auto coords_array (std::index_sequence&lt;Is...&gt;)  {

        auto dims = std::array {std::tuple_size_v&lt;Tuples&gt;... };
        for (std::size_t i=1; i&lt;dims.size(); ++i)  { dims[i] *= dims[i-1]; }

        std::array&lt;std::array&lt;std::size_t,dims.size()&gt;,K&gt; arr = {};
        for (std::size_t j=0; j&lt;arr.size(); ++j) {  arr[j] = { ((j*dims[Is]) / arr.size()) % std::tuple_size_v&lt;Tuples&gt;... };  }
        return arr;
    }

    static constexpr auto arr = coords_array(std::make_index_sequence&lt;N&gt;{});
};
</code></pre>
<p>Then, <code>arr</code> is an array of coordinates for each possible combination. Now, we have to put this in the compile time world since getting types from a tuple requires to know the index at compile time. We define a <code>get</code> method that will return the expected <code>std::tuple</code> holding <code>std::integer_sequence</code> types. Note that we need some intermediate steps for managing the loops through <code>std::integer_sequence</code> objects and also to handle the fact that we have to deal with two parameters packs of different sizes. The full <code>Coords</code> struct is as follows:</p>
<pre><code>template&lt;typename...Tuples&gt;
struct Coords {

    static constexpr std::size_t N = sizeof...(Tuples);
    static constexpr std::size_t K = (1*...*std::tuple_size_v&lt;Tuples&gt;);

    template&lt;std::size_t...Is&gt;
    static constexpr auto coords_array (std::index_sequence&lt;Is...&gt;)  {

        auto dims = std::array {std::tuple_size_v&lt;Tuples&gt;... };
        for (std::size_t i=1; i&lt;dims.size(); ++i)  { dims[i] *= dims[i-1]; }

        std::array&lt;std::array&lt;std::size_t,dims.size()&gt;,K&gt; arr = {};
        for (std::size_t j=0; j&lt;arr.size(); ++j) {  arr[j] = { ((j*dims[Is]) / arr.size()) % std::tuple_size_v&lt;Tuples&gt;... };  }
        return arr;
    }

    static constexpr auto arr = coords_array(std::make_index_sequence&lt;N&gt;{});

    template&lt;std::size_t j, std::size_t...Is&gt;
    static auto get (std::index_sequence&lt;Is...&gt;)
        -&gt; std::integer_sequence &lt;std::size_t,arr[j][Is]...&gt;;

    template&lt;std::size_t...Is, std::size_t...Js&gt;
    static auto get (std::index_sequence&lt;Is...&gt; i, std::index_sequence&lt;Js...&gt;)
        -&gt; std::tuple &lt;decltype(get&lt;Js&gt;(i))...&gt;;

    static auto get ()  {
        return get (std::make_index_sequence&lt;N&gt;{}, std::make_index_sequence&lt;K&gt;{});
    }
};
</code></pre>
<p>Now, we can define a <code>CartesianProduct</code> struct that will use <code>Coords</code>:</p>
<pre><code>template&lt;typename...Tuples&gt;
struct CartesianProduct {

    template &lt;std::size_t...Is&gt;
    static auto inner (std::integer_sequence&lt;std::size_t,Is...&gt;)
        -&gt; std::tuple&lt;std::tuple_element_t&lt;Is,Tuples&gt;...&gt;;

    template&lt;typename...Coords&gt;
    static auto outer (std::tuple&lt;Coords...&gt;)
        -&gt; std::tuple&lt;decltype(inner (Coords{}))...&gt;;

    using type = decltype (outer (Coords&lt;Tuples...&gt;::get()));
};
</code></pre>
<p>Actually, we manage here two loops through <code>outer</code> and <code>inner</code> methods. This solution should work with non default constructible class and works with C++17. With a small redesign, the <code>Coords</code> struct could probably be used in different use cases.</p>
<p>Example of use:</p>
<pre><code>using A = std::tuple&lt;int,char&gt;;
using B = std::tuple&lt;double,short,std::string&gt;;

static_assert (std::is_same_v&lt;CartesianProduct&lt;A,B&gt;::type, std::tuple &lt;
    std::tuple&lt;int,double&gt;,
    std::tuple&lt;int,short&gt;,
    std::tuple&lt;int,std::string&gt;,
    std::tuple&lt;char,double&gt;,
    std::tuple&lt;char,short&gt;,
    std::tuple&lt;char,std::string&gt;
&gt;&gt;);

static_assert (std::is_same_v&lt;decltype(Coords&lt;A,B&gt;::get()), 
std::tuple&lt;
    std::integer_sequence&lt;std::size_t,0,0&gt;,
    std::integer_sequence&lt;std::size_t,0,1&gt;,
    std::integer_sequence&lt;std::size_t,0,2&gt;,
    std::integer_sequence&lt;std::size_t,1,0&gt;,
    std::integer_sequence&lt;std::size_t,1,1&gt;,
    std::integer_sequence&lt;std::size_t,1,2&gt;
&gt;&gt;);
</code></pre>
<p><a href="https://godbolt.org/z/87YdE88Tz" rel="nofollow noreferrer">Demo</a></p>

Q: Flutter: Prevent deadlocks in a task queue when tasks enqueue other tasks A: <p>Maybe this code can handle it?<br />
It simply blocks the execution of the next (in the queue) code until current code releases the lock.</p>
<pre class="lang-dart prettyprint-override"><code>import 'dart:async';

import 'package:multitasking/multitasking.dart';
import 'package:multitasking/synchronization/reentrant_lock.dart';

Future&lt;void&gt; main() async {
  final a = Task.run(() =&gt; doSomething(1));
  final b = Task.run(() =&gt; doSomethingElse(2));
  final c = Task.run(() =&gt; doSomething(3));

  await Future.wait([a, b, c]);

  print('done');
}

final _myLock = ReentrantLock&lt;Object&gt;();

Future&lt;void&gt; doSomething(int count) async {
  return _myLock.lock(() async {
    await Future&lt;void&gt;.delayed(const Duration(milliseconds: 500));
    print('something #$count');
    await doSomethingElse(count);
  });
}

Future&lt;void&gt; doSomethingElse(int count) async {
  return _myLock.lock(() =&gt; print('something else #$count'));
}

extension&lt;T&gt; on ReentrantLock&lt;T&gt; {
  Future&lt;R&gt; lock&lt;R&gt;(FutureOr&lt;R&gt; Function() f) async {
    await acquire();
    try {
      return await f();
    } finally {
      await release();
    }
  }
}

</code></pre>
<p>Result:</p>
<pre class="lang-none prettyprint-override"><code>something #1
something else #1
something else #2
something #3
something else #3
done
</code></pre>
